## Call chain analysis
aaptjs exposes eight wrapper functions on its `index.js` module.exports -- `list`, `dump`, `packageCmd`, `remove`, `add`, `crunch`, `singleCrunch`, `version` -- and every one funnels through the same `promistify(cmd, callback)` helper at index.js:14, whose body constructs a Promise around `exec(cmd, (code, stdout, stderr) => {...})` at index.js:18:3. That `exec` is NOT a method on an imported module: it is the global `exec` injected by the module's very first statement, `require('shelljs/global')`, which copies every shelljs command onto the Node global object. shelljs.exec in turn wraps Node's child_process.exec, which is the function Jazzer.js's CommandInjection detector hooks via its before-call instrumentation. We pick `list(apkfilePath, cb)` as the entry point because its command template is the shortest, `${aapt} l ${apkfilePath}`, and the user-controlled suffix is interpolated directly into the shell string with NO quoting, NO escaping, and NO path.resolve/normalize pass. All other wrappers share the same no-escape pattern on their respective user-controlled argument (`apkfilePath`, `files` joined with ' ', `resource` joined with ' ', `inputFile`/`outputfile`), so any of them would reach the same sink; `list` is simply the smallest signature. Jazzer's child_process.exec before-hook from @jazzer.js/bug-detectors inspects the command string synchronously BEFORE the shell is spawned, so the detector fires on the first iteration whose apkfilePath carries the sentinel, even though exec itself is async and promistify catches `exec`'s error-code branch -- reportFinding stores the finding in per-iteration state that Jazzer checks at the end of the iteration regardless of whether the returned Promise is awaited.

## Argument reasoning
list()'s command string is `${aapt} l ${apkfilePath}`. apkfilePath is NOT wrapped in quotes, NOT passed through path.resolve, NOT lowercased, NOT shell-escaped -- the raw bytes we hand the fuzzer reach exec() verbatim. The Jazzer.js CommandInjection detector plants the ASCII sentinel `jaz_zer` via libFuzzer compare-trap dictionary and flags any child_process call whose command string contains that substring; `jaz_zer` has no shell metacharacter that would be rewritten anywhere upstream, so it survives through the single template literal unchanged. We use provider.consumeRemainingAsString() for maximum entropy and strip NUL bytes with .replace(/\u0000/g, "") because Node's child_process rejects commands containing NULs with TypeError [ERR_INVALID_ARG_VALUE] -- that TypeError is unrelated to the bug and starves libFuzzer of coverage signal if we let it fire. No other arguments to list() need to be fuzz-controlled: the callback is any function (we hand it an empty closure), and the exec on the backing shell will exit non-zero (the real aapt binary is bundled under /work/target/bin/linux/aapt but exits non-zero for a bogus path), which promistify catches and rejects -- harmlessly, because Jazzer already recorded the finding synchronously before exec returned.

